      "target": "../sections/sec-001-ii-definition-of-key-terms.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "General \nHIV refers to the human immunodeficiency virus. There are two types of HIV: HIV-1 and HIV-2.\nHIV-1 is responsible for the vast majority of HIV infections globally.\nAcute (HIV) infection is the period between a person being infected with HIV and HIV  \nantibodies being detectable by a serological assay.\nAge groups and populations\nThe following definitions for adults, adolescents, children and infants are used in these guidelines \nfor the purpose of implementing recommendations for specific age groups. It is acknowledged  \nthat countries may have other definitions under national laws:\n•",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Defines key terms used in the 2021 HIV guidelines, starting with HIV types and acute infection. It sets age-group definitions for adults, adolescents, children and infants, and defines key and vulnerable populations. Further definitions cover terms such as serodiscordant couples and partners.",
        "state": "draft",
