      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "\u0003Background and rationale",
      "pageStart": 16,
      "pageEnd": 18,
      "target": "../sections/sec-005-11-background-and-rationale.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "HIV infection is a public health issue. In 2019, more than 38.0 million [31.6 million–44.6 \nmillion] people were living with HIV, and more than 1.7 million [1.2 million–2.2 million] people \nacquired HIV. Nearly 61% of the people newly infected with HIV live in sub-Saharan Africa. \nBetween 2010 and 2019, the epidemic also continued to grow in eastern Europe and central \nAsia, with the number of people acquiring HIV rising by 72%. There were also increases in the \nMiddle East and North Africa (22%) and Latin America (21%) (1). The United Nations General \nAssembly agreed in June 2016 that ending",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Gives the epidemiological background, citing 38 million people living with HIV and 1.7 million new infections in 2019, with rising incidence in eastern Europe, central Asia and other regions. It notes that the 2020 Fast-Track targets were missed despite falling AIDS deaths. It then explains why updated guidance was needed on prevention, infant diagnosis, viral load monitoring and timing of ART for people with TB symptoms.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
