    {
      "id": "library/10665-340190/blocks/prose-sec-013",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Box 1. \u0003Defining “substantial risk”",
      "pageStart": 22,
      "pageEnd": 30,
      "target": "../sections/sec-013-box-1-defining-substantial-risk.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Substantial risk of HIV infection is provisionally defined as HIV incidence greater than \n3 per 100 person-years in the absence of PrEP. HIV incidence greater than 3 per 100 \nperson-years has been identified among men who have sex with men, transgender \nwomen and heterosexual men and women who have sexual partners with undiagnosed \nor untreated HIV infection. Individual risk varies within groups at substantial risk \ndepending on individual behaviour and the characteristics of sexual partners. Most of \nthe PrEP trials reviewed for the 2015 WHO recommendation identified and recruited \ngroups at",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Defines substantial HIV risk as incidence above 3 per 100 person-years without PrEP and discusses how thresholds and risk assessment tools may vary. The section then covers evidence and considerations for the dapivirine vaginal ring, as the start and end of this cut block indicate. Its closing part discusses switching between or combining the ring and oral PrEP, the need for adherence, and unknowns about whether the ring adds new users or replaces oral PrEP. It ends with cost and cost-effectiveness, calling for further analysis using real-world data.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
