      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "\u0003Point-of-care infant diagnosis",
      "pageStart": 34,
      "pageEnd": 40,
      "target": "../sections/sec-016-31-point-of-care-infant-diagnosis.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Recommendation\nPoint-of-care nucleic acid testing should be used to diagnose HIV among infants and \nchildren younger than 18 months of age.\n(strong recommendation; high-certainty evidence)\nBackground\nAlthough significant recent investments in improving the diagnostic networks, centralized \nlaboratories and sample collection networks have been made in most high-burden settings, \nclear improvements in access to infant testing and treatment initiation of infants have not \nincreased at the same rate. Substantial challenges and barriers remain. First, in 2019, only \n60% of infants received an HIV n",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Presents the strong recommendation to use point-of-care nucleic acid testing to diagnose HIV in infants and children under 18 months. It explains the need, citing low early infant testing coverage, long laboratory turnaround times and high mortality among untreated infants. The later part of this cut block covers implementation, such as integrating point-of-care tools into laboratory networks, training and same-day linkage to care. It ends with research gaps, including quality assurance and a possible dual-use test for diagnosis and viral load.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
