      "target": "../sections/sec-017-32-point-of-care-viral-load-testing.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Recommendation\nPoint-of-care viral load may be used to monitor treatment among people living with HIV \nreceiving ART. \n(conditional recommendation; moderate-certainty evidence)\nBackground\nIn 2016, viral load testing was strongly recommended as the preferred approach to monitor \ntreatment among people living with HIV, with the associated development of a treatment \nmonitoring algorithm to identify potential adherence challenges or drug resistance – with \nthe latter requiring people to switch to a second-line treatment regimen (6). There has been \nsignificant uptake of viral load testing since 2",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Presents the conditional recommendation that point-of-care viral load testing may be used to monitor people receiving ART. It describes the growth of viral load testing since 2016 and remaining barriers, and introduces new point-of-care technologies giving results within one to two hours. Implementation considerations include fitting the technology within the treatment monitoring algorithm, integrated laboratory networks and setting patient priorities given limited capacity. It notes that rapid results may help prevent drug resistance, especially in children and adolescents.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
