    {
      "id": "library/10665-340190/blocks/prose-sec-019",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "\u0003Treatment monitoring algorithm",
      "pageStart": 46,
      "pageEnd": 53,
      "target": "../sections/sec-019-33-treatment-monitoring-algorithm.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Background\nViral load testing was strongly recommended as the preferred approach to monitor treatment \namong people living with HIV in 2016, with the associated development of a treatment \nmonitoring algorithm to try to identify those who need to switch to second-line treatment if \ndrug resistance is suspected (6).\nSince the 2016 viral load algorithm was developed, ART programmes in low- and middle-\nincome countries have undergone changes that have altered the clinical ART context \nconsiderably. Two key programmatic shifts include rapid ART initiation (often on the same \nday as the diagnosis o",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains the updated treatment monitoring algorithm in WHO's HIV guidance, noting that rapid ART initiation, the move from efavirenz-based regimens to dolutegravir and rising pretreatment NNRTI resistance have changed the context since the 2016 viral load algorithm. It reviews four questions: when to do the first viral load test, when to repeat it after an elevated result, whether to switch NNRTI-based regimens after a single test, and what threshold defines treatment failure. Supporting evidence is set out for each consideration. The section ends with research gaps, including the clinical relevance of low-level viraemia on dolutegravir and monitoring needs for children, adolescents and pregnant and breastfeeding women.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
