      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "\u0003Timing of ART for adults and children with TB",
      "pageStart": 59,
      "pageEnd": 61,
      "target": "../sections/sec-023-41-timing-of-art-for-adults-and-children-with-tb.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Recommendation\nART should be started as soon as possible within two weeks of initiating TB treatment, \nregardless of CD4 count, among people living with HIV.a\nAdults and adolescents \n(strong recommendation, low- to moderate-certainty evidence)\nChildren and infants \n(strong recommendation, very-low-certainty evidence)\na. \t Except when signs and symptoms of meningitis are present.\nBackground\nTB remains the leading cause of mortality among people living with HIV, despite substantial \nscale-up of ART, accounting for 30% of the AIDS-related deaths reported in 2019 (1). Since \n2010, WHO has recommen",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Gives the recommendation that people living with HIV start ART as soon as possible within two weeks of starting TB treatment, whatever their CD4 count, except where meningitis is present. The background traces earlier WHO advice on ART timing during TB treatment and notes the lack of alignment with rapid ART guidance. Evidence summarized includes trials showing that immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome was not more frequent with dolutegravir or raltegravir than with efavirenz.",
        "state": "draft",
