      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Box 4. \u0003Importance of screening for signs and symptoms of meningitis",
      "pageStart": 61,
      "pageEnd": 65,
      "target": "../sections/sec-024-box-4-importance-of-screening-for-signs-and-symp.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "meningitis\nAmong people living with HIV with TB meningitis or other forms of meningeal infection \nsuch as cryptococcus, earlier ART is associated with more severe adverse events and \nincreased mortality with cryptococcal meningitis. For people living with HIV with TB \nmeningitis, immediate ART is associated with more severe adverse events compared \nwith initiating ART two months after the start of TB treatment.a\n•\tART should be delayed by 4–6 weeks of ART following initiation of treatment for \ncryptococcal meningitis. Use of steroids is not recommended.a\n•\tART should be delayed at least four w",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Box 4 explains that ART should be delayed in people with cryptococcal or TB meningitis, for 4–6 weeks and at least four weeks respectively, because early treatment raises adverse events and mortality. The section then extends the earlier-ART recommendation to children by extrapolation from adult evidence, given the high risks of delay in young children. It closes with research gaps on newer ARVs in TB coinfection, corticosteroids to prevent immune reconstitution syndrome and multi-disease adherence support.",
